    clinical:
      "<b>Why this matters in Indian practice.</b><br>" +
      "<b>When you find necrotic enteritis, look immediately for coccidiosis.</b> Open the whole intestinal tract, not just the affected segment: <b>examine the duodenal loop, the mid-gut and especially the caeca for the pinpoint white spots, ballooning, thickening and blood that indicate <i>Eimeria</i></b>, and make a mucosal scraping for oocysts. In practice the two conditions travel together, and treating the clostridium while ignoring the coccidiosis simply postpones the next outbreak. Review the anticoccidial programme, whether it is being rotated, and whether the litter is wet enough to be sporulating oocysts efficiently.<br><br>" +
      "<b>Ask about the ration as well.</b> Diets based heavily on <b>wheat or barley</b>, or containing generous <b>fish meal</b>, predispose to necrotic enteritis, and a recent sudden change of feed is a common trigger. Advise gradual feed transitions and, where the problem is recurrent, discuss the cereal base and protein source with whoever formulates the ration.<br><br>" +
      "<b>Use the processing plant as a diagnostic tool.</b> Subclinical necrotic enteritis kills nothing and is therefore invisible on the farm, but it damages feed conversion and produces cholangiohepatitis. <b>If a producer reports rising liver condemnations together with poor feed conversion and uneven flocks, investigate for subclinical necrotic enteritis and coccidiosis</b> even though no birds are dying. This is often a substantial and entirely recoverable economic loss.<br><br>" +
      "<b>Treat gangrenous dermatitis as evidence of immunosuppression.</b> When birds show dark, wet, crackling, gangrenous skin over the wing tips and breast, the skin lesion is the end of the story rather than the beginning. <b>Examine the bursa, thymus and bone marrow in the same birds</b> — a pale fatty marrow points to chicken infectious anaemia, an atrophic bursa to infectious bursal disease — and go and look at the feed store for mould. At the same time correct the mechanical causes: <b>wet litter, overcrowding, cannibalism, poor feather cover and rough handling</b>, all of which provide the breaks in the skin the organisms need.<br><br>" +
      "<b>In chicks dying in the first fortnight, look for the pasted vent and the yolk sac.</b> Pullorum disease remains present in backyard and small commercial flocks in India. <b>Chalky white pasty droppings pasting up the vent, with a retained unabsorbed yolk sac and grey-white nodules in the liver, gizzard and — very characteristically — the heart muscle</b>, make a strong presumptive diagnosis. Culture liver, spleen and yolk sac to confirm, and then look upstream: the source is an infected breeder hen.<br><br>" +
      "<b>Examine the ovary in every adult hen you post-mortem from a flock with chick losses.</b> The carrier hen is the reservoir for both pullorum disease and fowl typhoid, and her ovary tells the story: <b>misshapen, angular, discoloured grey-green or brown, cystic follicles hanging on stalks instead of the normal orange spherical hierarchy</b>. Finding this links the adult flock to the dying chicks and directs control to where it can work. Advise a <b>test-and-slaughter programme using the rapid whole-blood plate test in the breeder flock</b>, which is simple, cheap and can be done bird-side.<br><br>" +
      "<b>Recognise the bronze liver of fowl typhoid.</b> A growing or adult bird with a pale shrunken comb, greenish-yellow diarrhoea and, at post-mortem, an <b>enlarged bronze to mahogany-coloured liver with fine grey-white foci and an enlarged friable spleen</b>, has fowl typhoid until proved otherwise. This is still an important disease of Indian layer and backyard flocks.<br><br>" +
      "<b>Give accurate advice about eggs and human salmonellosis.</b> This is a genuine public-health responsibility. Explain that <b><i>Salmonella</i> Enteritidis gets inside the egg before the shell is formed</b>, so a clean-looking egg can still be contaminated and <b>washing the shell does not make it safe</b>. The measures that work at household level are <b>keeping eggs cool, cooking them thoroughly until both white and yolk are firm, avoiding raw or lightly cooked egg preparations for children, the elderly, pregnant women and anyone immunosuppressed, and washing hands and utensils after handling eggs and raw poultry</b>. At farm level, emphasise rodent control, clean heat-treated feed, and keeping wild birds out — rodents in particular are a persistently underestimated source of <i>Salmonella</i> in Indian poultry units.",

